n we  take combine the best of  systems that allow us to go very deep  into cellular phenotype and gene  expression  and genomics and epigenomics and so on  and put that into spatial context  well lucky for us there are a number of  solutions  emerging now in recent years to this  problem and i've  i've put examples of i guess four of the  most common  technologies here the first one is based  on  i guess an extension a quantitative  extension from existing methodologies so  multi-parameter immunofluorescence  so in the top left here we have a  section stained with multiple antibodies  on the vector system  there are other manifestations of this  technology like codex that uses barcode  and antibodies allowing you to increase  the  multiplicity of antibody density um  in the lower left is an example from the  read core platform which is a  commercialization of the physique  technology  this and other technologies like sigfish  essentially do in situ sequencing and so  this is a really  interesting novel solution to the  problem in the top right we have an  example of some data  from the 10x genomics vision platform  and so this is essentially  an array of capture probes across a  slide and it gives us  an array of um essentially  transcriptomes  and in the bottom right an example of  kind of a cartoon demonstrating how we  might use  the nanostream geomx platform which  allows us to do targeted  measurements so these are four really  kind of different approaches to the same  problem  and i think the reason we need so many  solutions is this is a complex space so  as you can imagine  in spatial analysis of a tumor we're  trying to solve multiple problems we  need  ultimately we need high resolution we  need  massive multiplexing we want to be able  to measure many many  proteins or the whole transcriptome for  instance we need it to be  sensitive so we need to be able to  measure not just highly abundant  molecules but also molecules that are  important to the biology of cells like  transcription factors  and finally we need these technologies  to work on  the kind of tissues that we typically  have access to and certainly in  translational  research that tissue is often formal and  fixed paraffin embedded  and so none of these platforms really  solve every one of these solutions and  so i think that's why we're very  fortunate to have  multiple solutions now um emerging  i'm going to spend a moment just  introducing the nanostring platform the  geomx dsp  which i think is a really fantastic  solution to  to this problem um and it works a little  differently to other platforms so  and given that we have a number of  presentations featuring it today i  thought i would  briefly introduce it so here i'm showing  you  a cartoon depicting the way that the  transcriptome assays work so on the left  here you can see  we have essentially a hybridization  probe binding to a target  mrna this probe is conjugated to a  barcoding oligo  via a uv cleavable linker so when you  hybridize these probes to your tissue  they bind to your to the tissue  and to the mrnas within it we can then  use  uv illumination to break the uv linker  in areas of interest  and then isolate those barcodes and  measure them  so this shows you roughly the workflow  here  starting at number one we stain slides  not only with a  with the library of these oligos but  also with antibodies that allow us to  get an immunofluorescent image to guide  where we uh do our studies we image the  slides select regions of interest we  then illuminate those regions of  interest  those barcodes are released they're  aspirated and then measured and in the  new  uh in the newest uh kits these  measurements are done through next  generation sequencing  and of course um as many of you will  heard from carl june's presentation this  technology can also be used by  conjugating barcodes  to antibodies so with that i'd like to  finish my introduction  welcome you all to this session on  sp