ast cancers that we've been  collecting for a number of years  across all the subtypes of disease these  tissues are dissociated  and every cell that we're able to  recover undergoes  single cell rna sequencing as well as a  suite of other methods including  sightseek  which i can elaborate more on in  question time if people are interested  and what this gives us then is a is a  really wonderful  taxonomy of the cells that are present  within a breast  tumor acknowledging of course that not  every technology captures every  cell so this is a very high level view  of the first snapshot  of about 30 cases here from this  early breast cancer cellular atlas and  you can see that  many of the cell types we know to be  present in  breast cancer are represented here and  when we  conduct really detailed annotation of  these cells we break this down  into a really detailed taxonomy and you  can see here  the major classes of epithelial immune  and stromal cells  breaking down into subsets of cell types  and ultimately  into states  from these cells  so i don't have time to talk about the  stromal or epithelial populations today  but  i want to talk briefly about the t cell  data as it  pertains to spatial profiling and so  here i'm showing you a umap clustering  of all the t  cells from our breast cancer atlas here  you can see that there are many diverse  phenotypes  present this also includes some innate  lymphoid cells  and k cells and so on but what's really  interesting is that we see a really  really wide diversity of phenotype of t  cells present within  breast cancers and in triple negative  breast cancers most of these states  interestingly  exist in every patient so it's not like  one patient has  type a and another patient has type b  most of these states are existing in  every one of the tumors that we observe  and while all of these states can be  detected in every subtype  interestingly there are three of them  that are enriched in triple negative  breast can